-Run configurations execute programs without breakpoints or stepping. Stop
-an executing program with **Tasks: Terminate Task** or its terminal's trash
-button. Program arguments still come from `goose.runArguments`.
+A configuration with `"noDebug": true` runs the program with the JIT,
+without breakpoints or stepping. Stop an executing program with **Tasks:
+Terminate Task** or its terminal's trash button. Program arguments still come
+from `goose.runArguments`. A `goose` configuration without `noDebug` debugs
+the program instead (next section); **Run Without Debugging** (Ctrl+F5 when
+`goose.useRunKeybindings` is off) runs any `goose` configuration with the JIT.
+
+## Debugging
+
+The extension debugs a Goose program through **goose-dap**, a Debug Adapter
+Protocol proxy in front of LLDB's `lldb-dap` that shows the program at the
+Goose level (`dap/goose-dap` in goose-debugger). It needs:
+
+- a compiler with `goose build -g` (`--debug-info`; `goose help build` lists
+  it). The extension checks before each debug session and says so if the
+  compiler in `goose.compilerPath` lacks it. **Run with JIT** works without it.
+- `goose-dap` on PATH, or `goose.debugAdapterPath` set to it, and Python 3,
+  which runs it.
+- an `lldb-dap`: on macOS the Command Line Tools have one; elsewhere install
+  LLDB, or set `goose.lldbDapPath` to the one to use.
+
+```json
+{
+  "goose.compilerPath": "${workspaceFolder}/build/Release/goose",
+  "goose.debugAdapterPath": "/path/to/goose-debugger/dap/goose-dap",
+  "goose.lldbDapPath": ""
+}
+```
+
+Start a session with **Goose: Debug** (the debug button in the editor title,
+or the Command Palette), or with the **Goose: Debug** configuration in the
+Run and Debug view and **F5** there. While `goose.useRunKeybindings` is on
+(the default), **F5** in a Goose editor still runs with the JIT, as before;
+turn it off to have F5 start debugging and Ctrl+F5 run with the JIT, as in
+other languages. A session builds the entry file (`goose.entryFile`, or the
+active Goose file) with `goose build -g` into an executable beside it, as
+**Build Executable** does, and starts it under the debugger. The build's
+output, and the compiler's error if it fails, are in the Debug Console.
+Breakpoints can be set in any `.goose` file; function breakpoints take Goose
+names (`area`, `geo::area`). The **Goose runtime errors** exception
+breakpoint, on by default, stops at a failed assert, bounds or overflow
+check, division by zero or `abort`, with the Goose frame that failed on top.
+
+A `launch.json` configuration can set what the session gets:
+
+```json
+{
+  "version": "0.2.0",
+  "configurations": [{
+    "type": "goose",
+    "request": "launch",
+    "name": "Goose: Debug",
+    "program": "${workspaceFolder}/main.goose",
+    "args": ["input.txt"],
+    "cwd": "${workspaceFolder}",
+    "env": { "LOG_LEVEL": "debug" },
+    "stopOnEntry": false
+  }]
+}
+```
+
+`program` is the Goose entry file, as for Run with JIT (the executable is
+built beside it). `args` defaults to `goose.runArguments` and `cwd` to the
+workspace folder. `buildArgs` adds `goose build` arguments, `lldbDap`
+overrides `goose.lldbDapPath`, `showRuntimeFrames` keeps the runtime's
+`gs_*` frames in the call stack, and `initCommands` runs LLDB commands after
+goose-dap's own. Anything else goes through goose-dap to `lldb-dap`. A JIT
+run cannot be debugged: debugging always uses a built executable. Debugging
+needs a trusted workspace, and the two paths above are not read from an
+untrusted one.
 
